  "query_preview": "..High pressure is building back into the area this\nevening following the weak trough that crossed Utah early this \nmorning. The trough did bring somewhat improved mixing owing to \ncooler air aloft, although a shallow, elevated inversion layer \nstill exists. With the return of high pressure, which will \nstrengthen into Thursday, expect inversions to strengthen once \nagain, producing some valley haze but otherwise benign weather. \n\nSoutherly flow will increase on Friday ahead of the next storm \nsystem. These winds may not be strong enough to mix out all valley\ninversions, but there will be a potential for improvement,\nespecially west of I-15. The storm system itself is still progged\nto arrive on Saturday as a relatively fast-moving open-wave\ntrough, bringing widespread valley rain and mountain snow to the\nforecast area. Models are coming into better agreement with this \nsolution, with only around 28% of ensemble members holding on to a\nweaker trough and drier solution. The NBM now has greater than \n0.2 inches of QPF over the mountains and I-15 corridor even at the\n25th percentile, compared with yesterday where little to no \nmeasurable precipitation was indicated at the 25th percentile. At \nthe 75th percentile, the NBM has many mountain areas exceeding 1 \ninch with valleys generally greater than 0.2 inches to upwards of \n0.75 inches. Snow amounts in the 4-8 inch range is currently\nforecast for the mountains.\n\nThis trough will be the first in what looks to be a fairly active\npattern which will continue into late next week. Following a brief\nbreak in the weather Sunday into Monday, models have another\ntrough moving into the area on Tuesday. This looks to be a\nslightly cooler system which has the potential to bring snow to \nat least the higher valleys (around 5000-6000 feet).",
